### 5. Five Responsive UI Issues Found Using Chrome DevTools

For this task, I used a web version of **Instagram** and tested different screen sizes using Chrome DevTools.

| No. | Responsive UI Issue                                          | Device Size          | Possible Fix                                                    |
| --- | ------------------------------------------------------------ | -------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- |
| 1   | Navigation icons become crowded on smaller screens           | Around 375px width   | Use a compact mobile navigation menu                            |
| 2   | Some text becomes too small to read comfortably              | Around 375px–480px   | Increase font size for mobile devices                           |
| 3   | Images or posts may take up too much horizontal space        | Small mobile screens | Use responsive image width such as `max-width: 100%`            |
| 4   | Buttons may appear too close together                        | Around 375px width   | Increase spacing and touch target size                          |
| 5   | Desktop sidebar takes too much space on tablet-sized screens | Around 768px width   | Hide, collapse, or reposition the sidebar at tablet breakpoints |

### Example of Responsive Fixes

The following types of CSS changes could help solve these issues:

* Use **media queries** for mobile and tablet screens.
* Use flexible layouts such as **Flexbox or CSS Grid**.
* Set images to resize automatically using responsive CSS.
* Collapse large navigation menus on smaller screens.
* Increase the size and spacing of buttons for touch devices.

